  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Direct and Time-Shifting Laplace Methods",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert delayed and piecewise continuous-time signals into one-sided Laplace-domain expressions by choosing between direct integration and the time-shifting property.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Direct and Time-Shifting Laplace Methods",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Direct and Time-Shifting Laplace Methods",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "One-sided Laplace starts from the same transform idea as the bilateral form but changes the energy window to the causal side only. For causal analysis, you use $\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t)\\}$, where the variable $t$ is restricted to $t\\ge 0$, so the lower integration bound is $0$ instead of $-\\infty$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_0^\\infty x(t)e^{-st}dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the base case, start from $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$. Write the transform directly as $\\int_0^\\infty e^{-t}e^{-st}dt$ and evaluate it exactly. If you can keep the lower limit and causal factor explicit, you can reduce mistakes on signs, convergence, and conditions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Then use the delay template only when the signal has an explicit delayed-causal form. For $x(t-t_0)u(t-t_0)$, the one-sided rule is to factor out the delay as $e^{-st_0}$ and then transform the base causal kernel: $x(t)u(t)$. This is valid when $t_0\\ge 0$ and the signal is causal (zero for $t< t_0$ in the shifted representation).",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t-t_0)u(t-t_0)\\}=e^{-st_0}\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t)u(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this section decision rule: if you can see a clean delayed-on-causal form like $x(t-t_0)u(t-t_0)$, use the shifting property; otherwise split by regions and integrate directly on $[0,\\infty)$. For this lesson, your immediate workflow is select method, execute in one line form, and keep the causal notation stable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{u(t-t_0)-u(t)\\}=\\frac{e^{-st_0}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Direct and Time-Shifting Laplace Methods",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Finding a One-Sided Laplace Transform by Direct Integration",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the one-sided Laplace transform of $x(t)=(2t+3)u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the one-sided definition directly because the signal is causal and already written as a polynomial times $u(t)$, so $\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_0^\\infty x(t)e^{-st}dt$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{(2t+3)u(t)\\}=\\int_0^\\infty (2t+3)e^{-st}dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set up the direct sum on the same $[0,\\infty)$ support: split the integral into a linear and a constant part so each piece is standard for one-sided transform tables.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{(2t+3)u(t)\\}=2\\int_0^\\infty t e^{-st}dt+3\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st}dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the constant piece, use the known one-sided result $\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st}dt=1/s$ for $s>0$. For $\\int_0^\\infty t e^{-st}dt$, use one-step integration by parts.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^\\infty t e^{-st}dt =\\left[-\\frac{t e^{-st}}{s}\\right]_0^\\infty +\\frac{1}{s}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st}dt=0+\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot\\frac{1}{s}=\\frac{1}{s^2},",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute both pieces and combine into one rational expression. This preserves the causal transform workflow used later for larger polynomials.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{(2t+3)u(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s^2}+\\frac{3}{s}=\\frac{3s+2}{s^2},\\quad s>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Direct and Time-Shifting Laplace Methods",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Applying Time Shifting to a Delayed Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute the one-sided transform of $x(t)=e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a delayed causal signal, written as $x(t-a)u(t-a)$; here $a=2$ and the base shape is $e^{-t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the one-sided time-shifting property rather than setting up a full integral from $2$ to $\\infty$, so the step stays reusable for any similar delayed term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t-a)u(t-a)\\}=e^{-as}\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t)u(t)\\},\\quad a\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match the template with $x(t-2)u(t-2)=e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)$. This means $a=2$ and $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)\\}=e^{-2s}\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-t}u(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the base transform and then attach the delay factor: $\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-t}u(t)\\}=1/(s+1)$ (from the standard one-sided exponential result).",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)\\}=e^{-2s}\\frac{1}{s+1}=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s+1},\\quad s>-1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: only the delay factor is new, so the example confirms that delayed causality is handled by multiplying the base one-sided transform by a pure $e^{-as}$ term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Direct and Time-Shifting Laplace Methods",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding a Piecewise Signal Transform by Splitting the Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t)\\}$ for $x(t)=\\begin{cases}1,&0\\le t<2\\\\3,&t\\ge 2\\end{cases}$ using the definition.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is a piecewise causal signal, so we keep the one-sided definition and write separate support intervals for $x(t)$: $x(t)=1$ on $[0,2)$ and $x(t)=3$ on $[2,\\infty)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_0^\\infty x(t)e^{-st}dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Split the integral at the breakpoint $t=2$, then use the two interval constants as independent integrands.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_0^2 e^{-st}dt+3\\int_2^\\infty e^{-st}dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Evaluate each piece with the standard one-sided exponential rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^2 e^{-st}dt=\\frac{1-e^{-2s}}{s},\\quad\\int_2^\\infty e^{-st}dt=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s},\\quad s>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Add the two contributions and simplify into one symbolic expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t)\\}=\\frac{1-e^{-2s}}{s}+3\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s}=\\frac{1+2e^{-2s}}{s},\\quad s>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This result keeps the same direct-integration template as the previous section, with one new wrinkle: interval splitting handles piecewise support cleanly and preserves causality in the limits.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Quick check: as $s\\to\\infty$, $\\mathcal{L}_+\\{x(t)\\}\\to 0$, so the result is consistent with a bounded positive-time signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Direct and Time-Shifting Laplace Methods",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Choosing Between Direct Integration and Time Shifting",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute the one-sided transform of $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)-e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)$ and show the chosen method for each term.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This signal contains one direct causal term and one delayed causal term, so the method split is intentional: use direct one-sided transform on $e^{-t}u(t)$ and time-shifting on $e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)-e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the non-shifted causal exponential, apply the base one-sided rule directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+1},\\quad s> -1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the delayed term, use the one-sided time-shift template with $a=2$ to avoid another full integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-(t-2)}u(t-2)\\}=e^{-2s}\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s+1},\\quad s> -1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Subtract the delayed contribution from the direct contribution, then keep one simplified result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s+1}=\\frac{1-e^{-2s}}{s+1},\\quad s> -1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Method rule to carry forward: use the shift property when a clean $x(t-a)u(t-a)$ pattern is visible; use direct integration when the signal does not match that pattern or is not factored for an immediate shift rewrite.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
